  {
    "id": "jose_ex3_note",
    "type": "comment",
    "z": "jose_ex3_tab",
    "name": "Setup: paste an RSA private key into the key configuration first",
    "info": "1. Generate a key pair: `openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -out private.pem`\n2. Open the *demo RSA private key* configuration, set *Source* to **PEM key or certificate** if it is not already, and paste the contents of `private.pem` into **PEM**. *Algorithm* stays `auto`, which resolves to `RS256`.\n3. Deploy, then click the inject button.\n\nThe same configuration signs (private part) and verifies (derived public part). A verifier elsewhere only needs the public key: `openssl pkey -in private.pem -pubout` produces a PEM you can paste into a key configuration of its own.\n\nExpected debug output: the compact token with header `alg: RS256`, `kid: demo-2026`, then the verified claims `{ sub: \"alice\", role: \"admin\", iss: \"https://issuer.example\", aud: \"example-api\", jti: <random UUID>, iat, exp }` and the protected header in `msg.header`.\n\nA token from another issuer or audience, or one signed with a different key, leaves on the *rejected* output with `msg.error.code`, `claim` and `reason` set.",
    "x": 250,
    "y": 40,
    "wires": []
  },
  {
    "id": "jose_ex3_inject",
    "type": "inject",
    "z": "jose_ex3_tab",
